Two edits. Nothing else in the file changes, nothing is reordered, and no other rule is touched.

(1) Replace the comment above `.approval-actions` and the `min-width` line inside `.approval-actions input` — currently at src/web/styles.css:385-403 — with the text below. The comment is nine lines, the same length as the `#main > *` comment that is the file's next longest.

--- OLD (comment)
/* The card's own min-content used to be this row: it is a nowrap flex row
   holding an <input> whose automatic minimum is its ~234px intrinsic width,
   and .approval-card is a grid item, so the track floored at ~389px and the
   page overflowed at 320px. min-width: 0 below lets the input shrink, and the
   wrap keeps both buttons reachable when that is still not enough. */
--- NEW (comment)
/* The card's own min-content used to be this row: a nowrap flex row holding
   an <input> whose automatic minimum is its ~234px intrinsic width, and
   .approval-card is a grid item, so the track floored at ~389px and the page
   overflowed at 320px. The wrap below is what lets the row shrink, so the input
   needs no automatic minimum — and must not have one: `flex: 1` is basis 0%, so
   at min-width 0 the field is the row's only shrinkable item and takes the whole
   deficit, measuring 59px at a 414px viewport — six characters of the reason
   being typed, against 232px before this ticket. Wrap plus floor is one
   constraint: the wrap is what makes the floor safe to declare. */

--- OLD (declaration)
.approval-actions input {
  flex: 1;
  min-width: 0;
--- NEW (declaration)
.approval-actions input {
  flex: 1;
  min-width: 16ch;

`min-width: 16ch` is the whole of the BUG-1 fix. `flex: 1` (basis 0%) plus `min-width: 16ch` makes the hypothetical main size 16ch, so at every viewport narrower than about 570px the row cannot fit input + Approve + Reject and `flex-wrap: wrap` fires, putting the field on a line of its own at the full card width and the two buttons on the line below. At 600px and above the row fits on one line and the field takes the remainder, exactly as it does today. Measured field width: 234 / 259 / 172px at 320 / 375 / 414, and 247 / 547 / 731px at 600 / 900 / 1100 — the last three identical to the commit under test.

(2) Add one declaration to the `.journal-drawer-body` rule, at src/web/styles.css:471, as its first line:

--- OLD
.journal-drawer-body {
  display: grid;
--- NEW
.journal-drawer-body {
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  display: grid;

This is the BUG-2 fix: the same property, the same value and the same reason as the four card classes, on the fifth surface that shows stored free text. `.journal-drawer-body` is `display: grid` and client.js fills it with `<p>` lines carrying `evidence_refs`, `memory_refs`, `critic_result`, `policy_decision_id` and `risk.worst_reasonable_case`, all of which are stored POSTed strings. `overflow-wrap` is inherited, so declaring it on the container reaches every line. Measured: body scrollWidth 1229 against clientWidth 271 / 326 / 365 at 320 / 375 / 414 before, and equal to clientWidth at all three after. No comment is added — the rule carries none today, the file's convention is to comment the reasoning once at the wrap rule, and the test comment states the rest.

Two edits.

(1) In the test `the approval action row can shrink, and the selectors below have something to match` (test/web/pages.test.js:1487), extend the leading comment block with seven lines recording BUG-1 and why the pin is being rewritten, and replace the three lines of the `.approval-actions input` assertion. The `flex-wrap: wrap` assertion above it and the three render assertions below it are unchanged.

--- OLD (append to the leading comment, after the line `// above, and the render proves both selectors have a card to match.`)
//
// #71 BUG-1: the first version of this pin required min-width: 0, which is the
// declaration that broke the field. flex: 1 is basis 0%, so with the automatic
// minimum removed the input is the row's only shrinkable item and took the whole
// deficit: 59px at a 414px viewport, six characters, against 232px on the base
// commit. The pin below states the property that is actually wanted — a readable
// floor — rather than the one that happened to fix the overflow.

--- OLD (assertion)
  const inputs = [...css.matchAll(/\.approval-actions input\s*\{([^}]*)\}/g)].map((m) => m[1]);
  assert.ok(inputs.length > 0, 'src/web/styles.css styles the approval reason input');
  inputs.forEach((body, i) => {
    assert.match(body, /min-width\s*:\s*0\s*;/, `the reason input rule ${i + 1} declares min-width: 0, or the input's ~234px intrinsic width floors the card and the page overflows again`);
  });
--- NEW (assertion)
  const inputs = [...css.matchAll(/\.approval-actions input\s*\{([^}]*)\}/g)].map((m) => m[1]);
  assert.ok(inputs.length > 0, 'src/web/styles.css styles the approval reason input');
  inputs.forEach((body, i) => {
    const widths = [...body.matchAll(/min-width\s*:\s*([^;]+);/g)].map((m) => m[1].trim());
    assert.deepEqual(widths.length, 1, `the reason input rule ${i + 1} declares exactly one min-width, or which one wins is a matter of order; it declared ${JSON.stringify(widths)}`);
    const floor = /^(\d+(?:\.\d+)?)(ch|rem|px)$/.exec(widths[0]);
    assert.ok(floor, `the reason input rule ${i + 1} floors the field at a readable width in ch, rem or px, not at a keyword or a percentage it cannot be measured against; it declared ${JSON.stringify(widths[0])}`);
    assert.ok(Number(floor[1]) >= 12, `the reason input rule ${i + 1} floors the field at 12 characters or more, or it collapses to six characters of what a human is typing: at a 414px viewport min-width: 0 measured 59px against this rule's 16ch; it declared ${JSON.stringify(widths[0])}`);
  });

The assertion states the property — a floor of at least twelve characters, in a unit that can be measured — rather than a literal, so a later change to 18ch or 160px is green and a regression to 0, to `auto`, or to a percentage is red. All four of those were run against this revision and each turns the suite red.

(2) Insert this test immediately after the test above closes and before the comment `// #71 finding 6: the header shows tenants.list()[0].name`:

// #71 BUG-2: the same free-text class of defect on a fifth surface. The wrap
// rule is scoped to four server-rendered card lists, and the journal decision
// drawer renders the same stored free text — evidence_refs, memory_refs,
// policy_decision_id, worst_reasonable_case — by a different route, client.js
// filling #journal-drawer-body from GET /v1/decisions/:id. Nothing rendered it
// in any test, so nothing measured it: with 120-character unbreakable values
// the body laid out 1229px inside 271px at a 320px viewport, and the page only
// stayed the right width because #journal-drawer is position: fixed and so is
// left out of document scroll width. The text was reachable by scrolling the
// drawer sideways, which its overflow-y: auto makes overflow-x: auto.
//
// Unlike the card rule this one cannot be cross-checked against rendered
// markup: the drawer body is empty in the server's HTML by design, so there is
// no render in which the selector is known to match. That gap is the reason
// the pin is a declaration pin and is worth stating rather than hiding.
test('the journal decision drawer wraps the stored free text it is filled with', () => {
  const css = readFileSync(new URL('../../src/web/styles.css', import.meta.url), 'utf8');
  const bodies = [...css.matchAll(/\.journal-drawer-body\s*\{([^}]*)\}/g)].map((m) => m[1]);
  assert.ok(bodies.length > 0, 'src/web/styles.css defines a .journal-drawer-body rule');
  bodies.forEach((body, i) => {
    const values = [...body.matchAll(/overflow-wrap\s*:\s*([^;]+);/g)].map((m) => m[1].trim());
    assert.deepEqual(values, ['anywhere'],
      `.journal-drawer-body rule ${i + 1} declares overflow-wrap: anywhere and nothing else, or a decision's stored refs scroll the drawer sideways instead of wrapping inside it: with 120-character unbreakable values the body measured 1229px of scrollWidth against 271px of clientWidth at a 320px viewport; it declared ${JSON.stringify(values)}`);
  });
});

Then run `node --check test/web/pages.test.js` and `node --test test/web/pages.test.js` before this file counts as done. Expected on this revision: 67 pass / 0 fail, up from 66, and `node --test` 307 pass / 0 fail. Both test names are apostrophe-free, matching every existing test name in the file.

review.detail:
The rewritten "no later override" check is blind to an override nested inside an at-rule, because the rule parser `([^{}]+)\{([^}]*)\}` cannot balance braces. The check is correctly scoped to selectors naming a card class (the .page-footer negative case is fixed and verified), but every other responsive edit in this stylesheet is an `@media` block, and a card-class override written that way is missed — including at exactly the ≤900px widths this ticket exists to fix. Today the loop is also vacuous: only two rules in src/web/styles.css declare overflow-wrap and both sit before the card rule, so the loop body never executes on the shipped file.

`test/web/pages.test.js:1466`

Evidence: Parsing the shipped src/web/styles.css with the test's own pipeline yields exactly two overflow-wrap rules: `{i: 13, sel: ".tenant-name"}` and `{i: 51, sel: ".experiment-card,
.opportunity-row,
.approval-card,
.learning-card"}`, so `rules.filter(r => r.index > card.index)` is empty and the `for` loop asserts nothing. Then in a scratch copy this was appended to src/web/styles.css: `@media (max-width: 900px) {
  .approval-card {
    overflow-wrap: normal;
  }
}` — `node --test test/web/pages.test.js` printed `# pass 66 # fail 0`. The parser captures the selector as `@media (max-width: 900px)` and the body as `.approval-card { overflow-wrap: normal; }`, so the body passes the `/overflow-wrap\s*:/` filter while `touched` (splitting the selector and filtering by CARD_CLASSES) comes out `[]` and `assert.deepEqual([], [])` passes. src/web/styles.css:478 and :489 already contain two `@media (max-width: 900px)` blocks, so this is the file's normal edit shape, not a contrived one.

Fix: Either make the parser at-rule-aware before filtering (strip `@media`/`@keyframes` wrapper rules into their inner rules, or parse with a brace-depth walk) so a nested `.approval-card { overflow-wrap: ... }` is seen as its own rule; or scope the check by a means that survives nesting — e.g. assert that no rule anywhere in the file, at any nesting depth, has a selector naming one of the four classes other than the card rule itself. As a minimum, add a `@media (max-width: 900px) { .approval-card { overflow-wrap: normal; } }` case to the AC-7 mutation matrix so the gap is recorded rather than silent.
